      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To compare the effects of amlodipine and slow release metoprolol on subjective symptoms and signs of ischaemia during bicycle ergometric exercise tests in patients with stable angina pectoris."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A randomized double-blind comparison of the two drugs in patients with documented coronary disease required to have at least three attacks of angina per week and to perform a symptom-limited exercise test with significant signs of ischaemia in the ECG."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Out of 127 patients, 117 completed the study. Both amlodipine and metoprolol significantly increased total exercise time, total workload, time to onset of angina and time to 1 mm ST-depression with no significant differences between the drugs. Amlodipine was significantly more efficient than metoprolol in reducing ST-depression at maximum workload. Diary data revealed no differences in patients' self-rating of drug effects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Judged by suppression of subjective symptoms and performance on exercise tolerance tests amlodipine represents a useful alternative to metoprolol as monotherapy in stable angina pectoris."
        }
      ]},
